{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-67512",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=11$ and $n=4,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}, \\\\ {a}_{11} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{11-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{4}=27$ and ${a}_{11}=-\\frac{1}{81},$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{3}=27 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{10}=-\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{10}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{10}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{10}}{{r}^{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187} \\\\ {r}^{7} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
